# What a brain is allowed to make this machine do. One verb per line:
#
#   verb | args | description
#
# This is the whole surface. oal-brain-do refuses anything not on this list, and each verb validates
# its own arguments in default/brain/lib.sh -- a theme has to be an installed theme, an application
# has to be an installed desktop entry. An allowlist of command names without that check is a
# formality: "open" would become "run anything" the first time someone passed it a path.
#
# Adding a verb is a security decision, not a feature decision. The question is not whether a brain
# would find it useful -- it is what the worst plausible misuse looks like, and whether you would
# still want it then. An agent that can retheme your desktop is a good demo; one that can reinstall
# your bootloader is a support incident.

state  | none                | Report the theme, layout, agent posture, host and uptime
theme  | <name>              | Set the desktop theme to an installed theme
notify | <summary> [body]    | Put a message on screen through the desktop's notification service
open   | <desktop-id>        | Open an installed application by its desktop entry id
